  "subtitle": "[ASSET: left | DecorativeAccessory | width=12%] This month's VMA goes to [LINK:1964:DD_1|DD #1|2027-05-24], where on page 19, we learn that secretary Karen Page is considered nothing more than the law office of Nelson and Murdock's \"[SPAN: severity=info; text=most decorative accessory].\"\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Whoa!]\n\n[ASSET: right | JustAWoman | width=12%] But an Honorable Mention goes to this month's [LINK:1964:TOS_53_iron0|TOS #53|2027-06-13], where we learn that Iron Man <i>allowed</i> the Black Widow to escape after last month's adventure, with the reason given being:\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=\"After all, she <i>is</i> just a woman...!\"]&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(Wow!)]",
  "review_text": "This month featured eight graded stories, spread out over seven books (<i>Strange Tales</i> providing its normal two, as always), yielding a final weighted average of [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text=x.xx],\n\n\nwhich was !!!!!!!!!!!!!! than last month's  <b>5.93</b>.\n\n\n<i>Daredevil</i> #1 was the clear stand-out this month.&nbsp; Highlights include the introduction of this fascinating new super-hero, the introduction of the Enchantress, Spider-Man being unmasked (with it not being an \"imaginary story\"), the FF and Avengers team-up to take down the Hulk, the first Human Torch/Iceman team-up, and the origin of the Watcher.\n\nThe melding of the Marvel Universe has never been so much on display in a single month before, and I know the whole notion of the shared universe must have been such an exciting thing for the readers of the day.\n\nOutside of the <i>Daredevil</i> issue, there was no outstanding issue this month (even ASM was only \"fine\" this time around), and a couple of other sub-par offerings in normally reliable titles contributed to this month being lower than it looked like it was heading at first.&nbsp; But outside of the disappointing <i>Tales of Suspense</i> issue, there really were no unreadable stories, so overall, I think it improved this month.\n\nAs an observation only, it's clear that the list of regular pencillers available to Marvel seem to consist of Jack Kirby, Steve Ditko, Don Heck, Dick Ayers and Larry Lieber (who only does back-up stories), with an occasional assist or guest spot here and there (e.g. Bill Everett on <i>Daredevil</i> this month).&nbsp; As for writers, it continues to look like Stan Lee provides <i>all</i> the plots, with scripters often being needed to actually put words on the page.&nbsp; Obviously the various Marvel artists participate in the plot of the story as well when they draw out the action and guide the characters along - per the \"Marvel Method.\"\n\n&lt;begin rant&gt;\nI will go on early record here to point out that the people who criticize Stan Lee and believe that Jack Kirby and Steve Ditko deserve equal share in the establishment of the early Marvel success are misguided.&nbsp; I <i>do</i> indeed believe that Kirby and Ditko played a <i>huge</i> role in the creation of the mainstay Marvel characters - Kirby in particular with his outstanding story-telling techniques and interesting ideas - and that Stan Lee <i>absolutely</i> could not have done it without them.&nbsp; (I state that to be <i>very</i> clear here.)\n\nBut only one guy was overseeing everything, working with <i>all</i> the artists and keeping watch over the <i>entire</i> operation, and that guy was Stan Lee.&nbsp; Do I believe he took credit for more than he was probably deserving of?&nbsp; Yes, I do, particularly with the notion of \"co-creators\" - I think he should have verbally acknowledged that he was <b>co</b>-creator and not <b>sole</b> creator.&nbsp; I think he struggled to understand that in the end.&nbsp; But time and again, he <b>co</b>-created characters with <i>this</i> artist and <i>that</i> artist, and the characters still got created without any one artist able to reasonably claim (retroactively) all, or most, of the credit.\n\nBut there is no <i>question</i> that the Marvel Universe would not exist were it not for him.&nbsp; No matter who was co-creator, Stan Lee was always involved somehow.&nbsp; He was the one driving force behind it all, and anyone who suggests otherwise just hasn't read these old books, I suspect.&nbsp; At the <i>moment</i> (early in 1964), the number of artists is rather minimal, but it won't be long before he's juggling stories with Gene Colan, John Romita, and others that follow even them - go listen to what <i>they</i> have to say (along with Don Heck) - the whole problem revolves about Kirby and Ditko, with not many others having much to say.\n\nStan was the main writer, period, and to suggest that he would never have been able to do it with different artists seems short-sighted.&nbsp; <i>Yes</i>, they were exceptionally talented, but you don't have to look beyond what Stan did with the <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> (after Ditko left) to see that the quality didn't drop just because he had to start working with a <i>different</i> talented artist (John Romita).&nbsp; The book's popularity continued to soar despite the loss of one of its co-creators.\n\nRoy Thomas has gone on record as well as saying that Stan operated in such a way as to keep the artists working so they could get paid (implying that it freed him up also so that he could then turn to work with the next artist and make sure everything kept moving forward - all the while knowing that his typewriter awaited him once he had the pages in hand).&nbsp; And Flo Steinburg also remembers the plotting sessions that Stan and Jack would have in the office, so they <i>clearly</i> worked together.\n\nIt irritates me to see Stan so denigrated in the various writings and articles that have been put forth since his death.&nbsp; Many of them seem to paint Stan as some kind of monster, which seems preposterous - those characters didn't create themselves, and those stories didn't write themselves either!\n&lt;/end rant&gt;\n\n\n\n[DEVNOTE: *** SUMMARY_BEGIN ***\n*** THIS MONTH ***8 graded stories over 7 issues...TOTAL WEIGHT: 5.58 grade character issue    weight 9.2   dd        DD #1    1.00 6.0   asm       ASM #12  1.00 5.5   thor      JIM #103 0.50 5.0   ff        FF #26   1.00 5.0   torch     ST #120  0.50 3.5   giant     TTA #55  0.75 3.0   iron      TOS #53  0.50 2.5   strange   ST #120  0.335.45*** LAST MONTH ***9 graded stories over 8 issues...TOTAL WEIGHT: 6.33 grade character issue    weight 8.5   ff        FF #25   1.00 8.0   aven      AVEN #4  1.00 7.5   asm       ASM #11  1.00 6.5   xmen      XMEN #4  1.00 4.5   iron      TOS #52  0.50 4.0   thor      JIM #102 0.50 3.5   strange   ST #119  0.33 1.8   giant     TTA #54  0.50 1.5   torch     ST #119  0.505.93\n*** SUMMARY_END ***]",
  "letters_page": "[HANG0] * Introduction of Daredevil, Foggy Nelson, Karen Page, Enchantress, Executioner, and Captain Barracuda.\n[HANG0] * Origin of the Watcher (as provided in the brief backup tale inside of [LINK:1964:TOS_53_iron0|TOS #53|2027-06-13])\n\n[DEVNOTE: *** INTRODUCTION_BEGIN ***\nDaredevil | Milestone: (first)\nFoggy Nelson | Milestone: (first)\nKaren Page | Milestone: (first)\nNotes | first | Nelson and Murdock Law Firm\nNotes | first | flashback | death | Battling Murdock\nNotes | first | death | villain | Fixer\nNotes | first | villain | Slade\nEnchantress | villain | Milestone: (first)\nExecutioner | villain | Milestone: (first)\nCaptain Barracuda | villain | Milestone: (first)\nNotes | first | villain | House of Shadows\nNotes | first | flashback | Ikor, Uatu's father, leader of the Watcher High Council (in Tales of the Watcher)\nNotes | first | flashback | Emnu, a member of the Watcher High Council (in Tales of the Watcher)\n*** INTRODUCTION_END ***]\n\n<b>Roundup:</b>\n[DEVNOTE: *** HIGHLIGHTS_BEGIN ***]\n[HANG0] * Matt Murdock becomes Daredevil, but it's Battling Murdock's last stand that leaves the biggest impression.\n[HANG0] * Thor gains two memorable new foes, but the real surprise is discovering that Asgard extends beyond Odin's throne room.\n[HANG0] * The Human Top learns more about Hank and Jan's relationship in five minutes than Hank has in months.\n[HANG0] * FIXME - write something\n[HANG0] * The Hulk, Fantastic Four, and the Avengers finally collide and accidentally demonstrate that crossover spectacle and strong plotting aren't the same thing.\n[HANG0] * The Human Torch and Iceman's long-requested first meeting relies on plenty of coincidence, but Captain Barracuda proves more capable than expected.\n[HANG0] * A skeptical reporter, a living haunted house, and Dr. Strange all collide in a listless mystery that ends almost as soon as it begins.\n[HANG0] * The Watcher's origin earns its keep as backup insurance for a surprisingly weightless Iron Man adventure.\n[DEVNOTE: *** HIGHLIGHTS_END ***]",
  "ownership": "[HANG0] * The Avengers have to avoid getting hot foot\n[HANG0] * Thor faces large menaces where they don't belong\n[HANG0] * Giant-Man and Wasp meet a magician\n[HANG0] * Magneto is back again, and the Angel is in trouble!\n[HANG0] * The Human Torch has another encounter with the Plantman (<i>oh... joy</i>)\n[HANG0] * Dr. Strange visits a wax museum\n[HANG0] * Mandarin returns to pester Iron Man once again\n[HANG0] * A mystical new nemesis is gunning for Spider-Man\n[HANG0] * And speaking of mystical, Dr. Strange appears <i>again</i> to lend the FF a hand against the Sub-Mariner!"
